# Family defaults — Django + Vue + Docker Compose (dashboard_template family)
#
# LOADED, NOT COPIED. A project's .context/worktrees/config.env declares
# `WT_PROFILE="django-vue-compose"` and worktree.sh sources this file FIRST, then
# the project's config.env on top. So the project overrides anything here, and an
# improvement here reaches every project that declares the profile — including
# ones set up months ago.
#
# That is the whole point of the split. Before it, the profile was a file you
# copied: `worktree.sh` read only `config.env` and never looked at the profile
# again, so a fix landing here reached exactly zero existing projects. Fifteen
# projects each holding their own drifted copy of "the family default" is the
# same failure the shipped mechanism was built to end, one level up.
#
# Nothing project-specific belongs in this file. Its companion,
# django-vue-compose.project.env, is the half that must be filled per project.

# Services the isolated stack starts. This is a FLOOR, and worktree.sh now
# refuses to start when a project leaves it at the floor incorrectly — the
# override is no longer left to care.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-08-21). This comment used to read "qcluster/worker/
# backend-test stay out of the default set: they are started on demand by their
# own profiles". That is false for `qcluster`, which carries NO `profiles:` key
# in either project that has it, and the sentence is how two of the three field
# projects with an always-on worker shipped without one. Cost: a worktree that
# ran five hours with a non-empty queue and no worker, while every port check
# passed. A comment can assert anything about a compose file; nothing compared
# the two, so now something does.
#
# Derive it, do not copy it. `docker compose config --services` with NO
# --profile flag returns exactly the profile-less set — what a bare `up -d`
# starts, i.e. what dev runs. That is the set WT_SERVICES must cover:
#
#   docker compose config --services        # -> WT_SERVICES (or _EXCLUDE)
#
# `restart: unless-stopped` is corroborating evidence, not the test: a
# profile-less service with no restart policy is still started by dev.
WT_SERVICES="db backend"

# The other two classes. Both are OPTIONAL, both are "<names> # <reason>", and
# worktree.sh refuses a value with no reason — an escape nobody has to justify
# becomes the default.
#
#   WT_SERVICES_BY_HOOK  profile-gated services that legitimately run in a
#                        worktree. An ALLOWLIST for the running check, never a
#                        requirement: in this family it is `backend-test`,
#                        started by the E2E run (playwright globalSetup), NOT by
#                        WT_POST_CMD — `./test-e2e.sh --setup-template` uses
#                        `docker compose run --rm backend` throwaways and starts
#                        no long-lived service. Requiring it would fail every
#                        fresh create.
#   WT_SERVICES_EXCLUDE  profile-less services deliberately NOT started here.
#
# Anything in neither is on-demand: nobody starts it, and the per-project guide
# lists it with its --profile flag.
WT_SERVICES_BY_HOOK=""
WT_SERVICES_EXCLUDE=""

# The variable the compose file interpolates into every container_name, so a
# second stack does not collide on a name. Compose files in this family must
# carry `container_name: <name>${WT_SUFFIX:-}` — verify with
# `check-compose-isolation.sh` before trusting this profile.
WT_SUFFIX_VAR="WT_SUFFIX"

# Slot geometry. The stride must EXCEED the span between the lowest and highest
# port base; worktree.sh asserts it at startup rather than leaving it to care.
WT_PORT_STRIDE=10
WT_MAX_SLOTS=9

# Ready when the isolated Postgres answers over TCP. Deliberately NOT the unix
# socket: on a first run the entrypoint runs initdb and starts a temporary
# server that answers on the socket only, so a socket probe passes against a
# server that is about to shut down and the seed then dies mid-connection.
WT_READY_CMD='docker compose exec -T db psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U "$WT_DB_USER" -d "$WT_DB_NAME" -tAc "select 1"'

# Migrations, not a dump of dev. A schema-only start is seconds; cloning dev's
# data is minutes and couples every worktree to whatever dev happens to hold.
# Projects that genuinely need dev's data can override this with a pg_dump pipe.
WT_SEED_CMD='docker compose exec -T backend python manage.py migrate --no-input'

# Isolated E2E, included by default — provisions the E2E template database
# inside the worktree's OWN Postgres.
#
# PRECONDITION — the project's `test-e2e.sh` must read its ports from the
# environment (`DB_PORT=${DB_PORT:-<dev>}`), must source the `.env` worktree.sh
# writes into the worktree root BEFORE those defaults apply (or `:-<dev>` wins
# and an isolated run silently drives dev's database), must export `E2E_DB_PORT`
# for the Playwright global-setup, and must not set COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME itself.
#
# A script that hardcodes dev's values can only drive dev, which is why projects
# used to `sed` a per-worktree copy of it — and that generated copy is what
# shipped bare-slug compose projects no sweep could attribute to a worktree. One
# recovered copy carried `lang-taxonomy`'s slot config under a directory named
# `quickwins`: the generator had cloned a previous worktree's copy, not the root.
#
# Also check the Django e2e settings guard: it allowlists database names, and a
# project renamed without updating it refuses to boot against its own E2E DB.
# (work_hours uses `work_hours_test`, not the family's `_e2e` — check yours.)
WT_POST_CMD='./test-e2e.sh --setup-template'

# Stop the half of the stack Docker does not own, before the Docker teardown.
# In this family the frontend (and often the backend) runs as a HOST process
# started by dev.sh, not as a container — so `worktree.sh down` used to report a
# clean teardown while a vite/runserver kept holding the slot's port and the
# worktree directory it had been started from, which then could not be removed.
#
# `./dev.sh --stop` is safe to run here and was verified before shipping it as a
# default: it reaps by project path rather than a global `pkill vite`, which
# would take the main tree's dev server and every sibling project's with it, and
# its PROJECT_ROOT resolves to the WORKTREE even when dev.sh is a symlink into
# it — so a worktree teardown cannot stop dev's own processes.
#
# Probed, not assumed. All five current adopters of this profile support
# `--stop`, but 4 of the 14 dev.sh scripts on this machine do not — and those
# four parse no arguments at all, so an unknown flag is ignored and the script
# STARTS the stack. Handed that dev.sh, an unguarded default would spin dev
# servers up as the first act of a teardown: not a failed hook, the exact
# inverse of the intended effect. The probe reads the script's own text, which
# is the only capability signal a shell script offers.
#
# Failure here is reported and non-fatal, so the worst case is a warning and the
# old behaviour, never a blocked teardown.
WT_PRE_DOWN_CMD='if grep -q -- "--stop" ./dev.sh 2>/dev/null; then ./dev.sh --stop; else echo "dev.sh does not support --stop; host processes are NOT being stopped"; fi'
